WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.980
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:09.540
It’s time for some fluid simulations again!
Writing fluid simulations is one of the most

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:15.120
fun things we can do within computer graphics,
because we can create a virtual scene, add

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:20.749
the laws of physics for fluid motion, and
create photorealistic footage with an absolutely

00:00:20.749 --> 00:00:27.150
incredible amount of detail and realism. Note
that we can do this ourselves, so much so,

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:32.369
that for this scene, I ran the fluid and light
simulation myself here at the Two Minute Papers

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:35.140
studio, and, on consumer hardware.

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:40.580
However, despite this amazing looking footage,
we are not nearly done yet! There is still

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:46.180
so much to explore! For instance, a big challenge
these days is trying to simulate fluid-solid

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:50.920
interactions. This means that the sand is
allowed to have an effect on the fluid, but

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:56.991
at the same time, as the fluid sloshes around,
it also moves the sand particles within. This

00:00:56.991 --> 00:00:59.620
is what we refer to as two-way coupling.

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:04.369
We also know that there are different kinds
of two-way coupling, and only the more advanced

00:01:04.369 --> 00:01:09.789
ones can correctly simulate how real honey
supports the dipper and there is barely any

00:01:09.789 --> 00:01:15.219
movement. This may be about the only place
on the internet where we are super happy that

00:01:15.219 --> 00:01:17.090
nothing at all is happening.

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:23.560
However, many of you astute Fellow Scholars
immediately ask, okay, but what kind of honey

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:28.850
are we talking about? We can buy tens, if
not hundreds of different kinds of honey at

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:34.259
the market. If we don’t know what kind of
honey we are using, how do we know if this

00:01:34.259 --> 00:01:39.310
simulation is too viscous, or not viscous
enough? Great question!

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:44.700
Just to make sure we don’t get lost, viscosity
means the amount of resistance against deformation,

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:51.100
therefore, as we go up, you can witness this
kind of resistance increasing. And now, hold

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:55.829
on to your papers because this new technique
comes from the same authors as the previous

00:01:55.829 --> 00:02:02.389
one with the honey dipper, and enables us
to import our real-world honey into our simulation.

00:02:02.389 --> 00:02:08.539
That sounds like science fiction. Importing
real-world materials into a computer simulation?

00:02:08.539 --> 00:02:14.849
How is that even possible? Well, with this
solution, all we need to do is point a consumer

00:02:14.849 --> 00:02:20.569
smartphone camera at the phenomenon and record
it. The proposed technique does all the heavy

00:02:20.569 --> 00:02:26.640
lifting by first, extracting the silhouette
of the footage, and then, creating a simulation

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:33.250
that tries to reproduce this behavior. The
closer it is, the better. However, at first,

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:38.920
of course, we don’t know the exact parameters
that would result in this, however, now we

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:43.870
have an objective we can work towards. The
goal is to re-run this simulation with different

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:50.620
parameter sets, in a way to minimize the difference
between the simulation and reality. This is

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:56.379
not just working by trial and error but through
a technique that we refer to as mathematical

00:02:56.379 --> 00:02:57.739
optimization.

00:02:57.739 --> 00:03:04.079
As you see, later, the technique was able
to successfully identify the appropriate viscosity

00:03:04.079 --> 00:03:09.060
parameter. And when evaluating these results,
note that this work does not deal with how

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:15.340
things look - for instance, whether the honey
has the proper color or translucency is not

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:21.700
the point here. What we are trying to reproduce
is not how it looks, but how it moves.

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:27.020
It works on a variety of different fluid types.
I have slowed down some of these videos to

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:33.099
make sure we can appreciate together how amazingly
good these estimations are.

00:03:33.099 --> 00:03:38.239
And we’re not even done yet! If we wish
to, we can even set up a similar scene as

00:03:38.239 --> 00:03:44.090
the real-world one with our simulation as
a proxy for the real honey or caramel flow.

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:49.430
After that, we can perform anything we want
with this virtual piece of fluid, even including

00:03:49.430 --> 00:03:54.879
putting it into novel scenarios, like this
scene which would otherwise be difficult to

00:03:54.879 --> 00:04:02.081
control, and quite wasteful, or even creating
the perfect honey dipper experiment. Look

00:04:02.081 --> 00:04:09.620
at how perfect the symmetry is there down
below! Yum! Normally, in a real-world environment,

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:14.971
we cannot pour the honey and apply forces
this accurately, but in a simulation, we can

00:04:14.971 --> 00:04:20.669
do anything we want! And now, we can also
import the exact kind of materials from our

00:04:20.669 --> 00:04:27.050
real world repertoire. If you can buy it,
you can simulate it. What a time to be alive!

